WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.380 --> 00:00:09.920
Not so long ago, the question “should we
teach patriotism in schools?” would have

00:00:09.920 --> 00:00:11.300
seemed a strange one.

00:00:11.300 --> 00:00:13.680
The answer would have been “of course!”

00:00:13.680 --> 00:00:19.141
Today, the role of patriotism in schooling is
controversial, generally supported on the right

00:00:19.141 --> 00:00:21.740
and greeted skeptically by many on the left.

00:00:21.740 --> 00:00:26.680
Those of us who embrace patriotism believe
that students need to appreciate the sacrifices

00:00:26.680 --> 00:00:31.525
so many have made to secure our freedoms
and all that unites as as Americans.

00:00:31.525 --> 00:00:37.200
Indeed, a healthy patriotism is inextricably
bound to American civic health.

00:00:37.200 --> 00:00:42.960
To skeptics, “patriotism” is a term freighted
with partisanship, tinged with xenophobia,

00:00:42.960 --> 00:00:46.200
and often viewed as a divisive rhetorical ploy.

00:00:46.200 --> 00:00:50.380
Regardless of these larger debates, though, I think
that most Americans would agree that students

00:00:50.380 --> 00:00:56.034
should learn about all what's worth cherishing
in our rich history, and also the many, many ways

00:00:56.034 --> 00:00:59.936
we can do better in extending and
upholding the American legacy.

00:00:59.936 --> 00:01:05.723
In the end, whether we call that “patriotism”
or something else may just be semantics.

00:01:06.801 --> 00:01:10.747
